5. Major Cybersecurity Threats

5.1 Ransomware and Multifaceted Extortion

Ransomware remains one of the most visible and operationally disruptive cyber threats. Modern ransomware campaigns frequently involve more than encrypting files. Threat actors may first obtain access, move through the environment, collect sensitive information and then use the stolen data to pressure the victim. This multifaceted-extortion model can create consequences even when backups prevent successful encryption.

Verizon's 2025 DBIR reported ransomware in 44% of analyzed breaches, an increase from the previous report. Google Cloud/Mandiant's 2025 ransomware analysis also found that vulnerability exploitation was confirmed or suspected in about one-third of incidents it analyzed, often involving VPNs and firewalls. [2][5]

Common entry points: exposed VPNs, stolen credentials, phishing, vulnerable perimeter devices and third parties.

Common progression: initial access → privilege escalation → discovery → lateral movement → data collection → extortion/impact.

Potential impact: downtime, loss of availability, data exposure, recovery expenses and reputational damage.

Key defenses: offline/immutable backups, MFA, network segmentation, EDR, least privilege, rapid patching and tested incident response.

5.2 Phishing and Social Engineering

Social engineering attacks exploit human trust rather than only technical weaknesses. Phishing can deliver malware, steal credentials or persuade a user to approve a fraudulent transaction. Attackers increasingly use personal information and AI-generated text to make messages appear credible. Voice calls, messaging platforms and mobile devices are also used, expanding the attack surface beyond email.

Microsoft's 2026 report states that user execution accounted for 30% of observed initial access and valid accounts for another 20% in its cited Defender Experts data. It also reports a major increase in ClickFix-style attacker-supplied commands executed on devices. [4]

Attack examples: credential harvesting, fake login pages, malicious attachments, QR-code lures, fake support messages and business-email compromise.

Why it succeeds: urgency, authority, familiarity, fear and convincing personalization.

Controls: phishing-resistant MFA where feasible, secure email gateways, user training, browser protection, verification procedures and reporting channels.

5.3 Vulnerability Exploitation and Zero-Day Risk

Vulnerability exploitation occurs when attackers take advantage of weaknesses in software, operating systems, network devices, applications or configurations. Internet-facing systems are particularly attractive because they can provide direct access without requiring an employee to interact with a malicious message.

Verizon reported a 34% increase in exploitation of vulnerabilities as an initial attack vector in its 2025 DBIR, with zero-day exploitation of perimeter devices and VPNs receiving significant attention. [2] Microsoft reported that nearly 40,000 CVEs were published in the first half of 2026 and observed the median time from vulnerability discovery in the wild to weaponization falling below 24 hours. [4]

Risk is highest for internet-facing, unpatched or unsupported assets.

Asset inventory is essential because organizations cannot patch systems they do not know exist.

Prioritization should consider exploitability, exposure, business criticality and available compensating controls.

Controls: continuous asset discovery, vulnerability scanning, patch management, secure configuration and segmentation.

5.4 Credential Theft, Account Takeover and Identity Abuse

Identity has become a central security boundary. Attackers may obtain passwords through phishing or infostealers, reuse credentials from unrelated breaches, steal browser session information or abuse valid accounts after an initial compromise. Once inside, legitimate credentials can make malicious activity resemble normal user behavior.

Verizon's credential-stuffing research associated compromised credentials with 22% of breaches reviewed in its 2025 DBIR analysis. It also found credential stuffing to be a substantial share of authentication attempts in the SSO data it studied. [6]

Common methods: password reuse, credential stuffing, phishing, infostealers, session theft and token abuse.

Potential impact: unauthorized access to email, cloud storage, source code, finance systems and sensitive data.

Controls: phishing-resistant MFA, password managers, conditional access, privileged access management, session monitoring and rapid credential revocation.

5.5 Malware and Infostealers

Malware includes malicious programs designed to steal data, disrupt systems, establish persistence or enable follow-on attacks. Infostealers are particularly useful to attackers because they can collect browser passwords, cookies, tokens and other information that can later be used to access additional systems.

In APAC, Verizon's 2025 DBIR reported malware in 83% of breaches in its regional dataset, up from 58% in the previous report, and identified email as a key distribution vector. Ransomware was present in 51% of APAC breaches in that dataset. [7]

Typical delivery: phishing attachments, malicious downloads, cracked software, fake updates and compromised websites.

Controls: endpoint detection and response, application allowlisting where practical, browser hardening, email filtering and patching.

Response priority: isolate affected endpoints, reset exposed credentials, investigate persistence and determine what data was accessed.

5.6 Supply-Chain and Third-Party Attacks

Organizations increasingly depend on cloud providers, SaaS platforms, software libraries, managed services, contractors and technology vendors. This interconnectedness creates a dependency risk: an attacker may compromise a trusted supplier and use that relationship to reach multiple customers.

Verizon reported that third-party involvement doubled to 30% of breaches in its 2025 DBIR. [2] ENISA's 2026 Threat Landscape similarly emphasizes that growing cyber dependencies expand the attack surface. [3]

Risks include compromised software packages, stolen vendor credentials, insecure integrations and vulnerable managed services.

Controls: vendor risk assessment, software bill of materials (SBOM), dependency scanning, least-privilege integrations, contractual security requirements and monitoring of supplier access.

5.7 Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) and Hacktivism

DDoS attacks attempt to make a service unavailable by overwhelming network, application or infrastructure resources. In geopolitical contexts, hacktivist groups may use DDoS as a visible disruption tactic against public or essential services.

ENISA's 2026 Threat Landscape reports that ideology-driven DDoS accounted for 82% of recorded events affecting public administration in its EU dataset, while public administration represented 32% of targeted organizations. These figures describe the ENISA reporting population and should not be treated as a global rate. [3]

Impact: service unavailability, customer disruption, emergency response costs and reputational effects.

Controls: DDoS protection services, rate limiting, CDN/WAF deployment, traffic baselining, resilient architecture and tested failover.

5.8 Cloud and SaaS Security Threats

Cloud environments concentrate identities, data and computing resources. Misconfigured storage, excessive permissions, exposed APIs, compromised credentials and insecure service-to-service trust can create high-impact attack paths. The shared-responsibility model also means that organizations must understand which security controls are provided by the cloud provider and which remain their responsibility.

Common risks: exposed storage, weak IAM, leaked API keys, excessive permissions, insecure APIs and compromised administrator accounts.

Controls: least privilege, strong IAM, MFA, secrets management, logging, continuous configuration monitoring and separation of production environments.

5.9 AI-Enabled Cyber Threats

AI is becoming a force multiplier for attackers and defenders. Threat actors can use AI to draft convincing phishing messages, automate reconnaissance, analyze stolen data, translate content, generate malicious code and support social engineering. The main security concern is not that AI creates every attack from scratch, but that it can reduce the time and expertise required to scale familiar attack techniques.

ENISA's recent threat research describes AI being used to enhance offensive activity and notes the emergence of stand-alone malicious AI systems. Microsoft reports AI use across vulnerability discovery, reconnaissance, phishing, malware, exploit development and post-compromise activity, including increasingly automated attack chains. [4][8]

AI-assisted phishing: higher-quality, personalized and multilingual lures.

AI-assisted reconnaissance: faster processing of public information and target data.

AI-assisted exploitation: faster vulnerability research and attack development.

AI-system risks: prompt injection, insecure tool access, excessive agent permissions and sensitive-data leakage.

Defensive response: apply existing identity, data, application and access controls to AI systems rather than treating AI as a separate security boundary.

