7. Attack Lifecycle Analysis

A useful way to understand the threat landscape is to map common techniques to the stages of an attack. The following simplified lifecycle demonstrates how separate weaknesses can be chained into one incident.

Reconnaissance – identify people, technologies, domains, exposed services and organizational relationships.

Initial access – use phishing, stolen credentials, exploitation or a compromised supplier.

Execution – run malicious commands, scripts or payloads.

Persistence – maintain access using accounts, scheduled tasks, services, tokens or other mechanisms.

Privilege escalation – obtain higher permissions through credential theft or software/configuration weaknesses.

Discovery and lateral movement – identify systems, accounts, shares and valuable data.

Collection and exfiltration – gather sensitive information and move it outside the organization.

Impact – encrypt systems, disrupt services, extort the organization, commit fraud or destroy/alter data.

This lifecycle demonstrates why point solutions are insufficient. Blocking one technique may not prevent an attacker from moving to another stage. Defense-in-depth reduces the probability that a single successful action will become a full organizational compromise.

